<h2>Airway Management</h2>
<blockquote>"Airway is everything. Without a patent airway, nothing else matters." — EMS teaching</blockquote>
<h3>Overview</h3>
<p>Airway management is the single most important skill in EMS. A patient who cannot breathe will die within minutes. Master these techniques.</p>
<h3>Airway Anatomy</h3>
<h4>Upper Airway</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Nose and mouth</strong> — Entry points</li><li><strong>Pharynx</strong> — Nasopharynx, oropharynx, hypopharynx</li><li><strong>Larynx</strong> — Vocal cords, epiglottis (the "lid")</li><li><strong>Epiglottis</strong> — Covers the trachea during swallowing</li></ul>
<h4>Lower Airway</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Trachea</strong> — Windpipe, ~10-12cm long</li><li><strong>Carina</strong> — Where trachea splits into bronchi</li><li><strong>Bronchi</strong> — Right (more vertical) and left main bronchi</li><li><strong>Bronchioles</strong> — Smaller branches</li><li><strong>Alveoli</strong> — Where gas exchange happens</li></ul>
<h4>Pediatric Differences</h4>
<ul><li>Larger tongue relative to mouth</li><li>Larger, more anterior epiglottis</li><li>Narrowest point is below the vocal cords (cricoid)</li><li>Smaller trachea — easily obstructed</li><li>Higher oxygen consumption</li></ul>
<h3>Airway Assessment</h3>
<h4>Signs of Adequate Airway</h4>
<ul><li>Speaking in full sentences</li><li>Normal breath sounds bilaterally</li><li>No stridor or wheezing</li><li>Normal respiratory rate and effort</li></ul>
<h4>Signs of Compromised Airway</h4>
<ul><li>Stridor (upper airway obstruction)</li><li>Gurgling (fluid in airway)</li><li>Snoring (partial obstruction)</li><li>Inability to speak</li><li>Use of accessory muscles</li><li>Paradoxical breathing</li><li>Cyanosis</li></ul>
<h3>Basic Airway Maneuvers</h3>
<h4>Head-Tilt/Chin-Lift</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Use:</strong> Opening airway in patients WITHOUT suspected spinal injury</li><li><strong>How:</strong> One hand on forehead (tilt back), two fingers under chin (lift)</li><li><strong>Effect:</strong> Lifts tongue off posterior pharyngeal wall</li></ul>
<h4>Jaw-Thrust</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Use:</strong> Opening airway in patients WITH suspected spinal injury</li><li><strong>How:</strong> Place fingers behind angles of mandible, push jaw forward</li><li><strong>Effect:</strong> Opens airway without moving the neck</li></ul>
<h4>Recovery Position</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Use:</strong> Unconscious, breathing patients</li><li><strong>How:</strong> Patient on their side, bottom leg extended, top leg bent, head tilted back</li><li><strong>Effect:</strong> Prevents aspiration, maintains airway</li></ul>
<h3>Airway Adjuncts</h3>
<h4>Oropharyngeal Airway (OPA)</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Use:</strong> Unconscious patients WITHOUT gag reflex</li><li><strong>How:</strong> Insert upside down, rotate 180° as it passes the tongue</li><li><strong>Sizing:</strong> Corner of mouth to angle of jaw</li><li><strong>Caution:</strong> Will cause vomiting in patients with gag reflex</li></ul>
<h4>Nasopharyngeal Airway (NPA)</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Use:</strong> Semi-conscious or unconscious patients (can use with gag reflex)</li><li><strong>How:</strong> Insert along the floor of the nostril, bevel toward septum</li><li><strong>Sizing:</strong> Tip of nose to earlobe</li><li><strong>Caution:</strong> Don't use with suspected basilar skull fracture</li></ul>
<h3>Suctioning</h3>
<h4>When to Suction</h4>
<ul><li>Visible blood, vomit, or secretions in airway</li><li>Gurgling sounds during breathing</li><li>Inability to clear airway spontaneously</li></ul>
<h4>Technique</h4>
<ul><li>Pre-oxygenate if possible</li><li>Insert suction catheter without suction applied</li><li>Apply suction while withdrawing (max 10 seconds)</li><li>Re-oxygenate between suction passes</li><li>Monitor for bradycardia (vagal stimulation)</li></ul>
<h3>Bag-Valve-Mask (BVM) Ventilation</h3>
<h4>When to Use</h4>
<ul><li>Apneic patients</li><li>Inadequate respiratory rate or tidal volume</li><li>Need for high-concentration oxygen</li></ul>
<h4>Technique</h4>
<ul><li><strong>E-C clamp technique:</strong> Thumb and index finger form "C" on mask, remaining fingers form "E" on jaw</li><li><strong>Rate:</strong> 10-12/min (adult), 12-20/min (child), 20/min (infant)</li><li><strong>Volume:</strong> Just enough to see chest rise</li><li><strong>Use OPA/NPA</strong> to improve seal and effectiveness</li></ul>
<h4>Common Problems</h4>
<ul><li>Poor mask seal → reposition, use two-person technique</li><li>Gastric insufflation → Sellick's pressure (cricoid pressure)</li><li>Inadequate ventilation → check for obstruction, reposition</li></ul>
<h3>Advanced Airway (Paramedic Level)</h3>
<h4>Endotracheal Intubation</h4>
<ul><li>Direct laryngoscopy with Macintosh or Miller blade</li><li>Tube placement confirmed by:</li><li>Direct visualization</li><li>End-tidal CO2 (EtCO2)</li><li>Bilateral breath sounds</li><li>Chest rise</li><li>Absence of epigastric sounds</li></ul>
<h4>Supraglottic Airways</h4>
<ul><li><strong>LMA (Laryngeal Mask Airway)</strong> — Easy to insert, good rescue device</li><li><strong>King LT</strong> — Esophageal-tracheal combitube</li><li><strong>i-gel</strong> — Gel-filled cuff, no inflation needed</li></ul>
<h4>Surgical Airway (Cricothyrotomy)</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Use:</strong> When all other methods fail ("can't intubate, can't ventilate")</li><li><strong>Landmarks:</strong> Cricothyroid membrane (between thyroid and cricoid cartilages)</li><li><strong>Technique:</strong> Vertical skin incision, horizontal membrane incision, insert tube</li></ul>
